Vendored upstream files, byte-for-byte copies of the module's files (the .txt
suffix only keeps the Go tool from compiling them):

  testdata/data_test.go.txt       <- data_test.go
  testdata/tree_test.go.txt       <- tree_test.go
  testdata/mth_test.go.txt        <- mth_test.go
  testdata/example_test.go.txt    <- example_test.go
  LICENSE-codenotary-merkletree   <- LICENSE

The program embeds these five files, pins each by SHA-256 (upstream.go), and
checks every verbatim block of upstream_data.go and upstream_tests.go against
them line for line on every run. To re-check the copies against the module
cache:

  M=$(go env GOMODCACHE)/github.com/codenotary/merkletree@v0.1.2
  for f in data_test.go tree_test.go mth_test.go example_test.go; do
    cmp "$M/$f" "testdata/$f.txt"
  done
  cmp "$M/LICENSE" LICENSE-codenotary-merkletree

The fixture this program checks (vectors/interop/codenotary-merkletree.json in
the truestamp_merkle repository) also holds hash values taken from
data_test.go (testRoots and testPaths), and prose (written by text.go and
main.go) that quotes short code fragments of the module, under the same
license.


Not included
------------

github.com/stretchr/testify (MIT), which the module's own tests import, is not
copied or vendored. internal/assert is original code that provides only the
five assertion functions the copied tests call (True, False, Equal, Len, Nil);
internal/testing and internal/quietfmt are original stand-ins for the standard
library's testing and fmt packages.
